WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:00.940
Good morning, John.

00:00:00.940 --> 00:00:04.320
In 2008, I made a video called The Anagramming Guide to the Presidential Elections,

00:00:04.320 --> 00:00:08.680
in which I anagrammed the names of presidential candidates and came up with funny things, and then

00:00:08.680 --> 00:00:12.480
like a week later, The Daily Show anagrammed the names of presidential candidates

00:00:12.480 --> 00:00:15.000
and I was like, [unintelligible]

00:00:15.000 --> 00:00:17.540
And mostly I was just excited that The Daily Show
thought I was worth stealing from

00:00:17.540 --> 00:00:20.780
but then on you being you because you're

00:00:20.789 --> 00:00:22.289
a writer and you have lots of writer

00:00:22.289 --> 00:00:23.850
friends were able to determine that, in

00:00:23.850 --> 00:00:25.920
fact, The Daily Show had written that

00:00:25.920 --> 00:00:28.949
segment before I wrote mine. People have

00:00:28.949 --> 00:00:31.050
ideas concurrently, all the time. And a

00:00:31.050 --> 00:00:32.189
certain number of people who had those

00:00:32.189 --> 00:00:33.960
ideas will actually execute those ideas,

00:00:33.960 --> 00:00:36.300
leading to a debate over who thought of

00:00:36.300 --> 00:00:37.890
it first. Really unproductive. And

00:00:37.890 --> 00:00:39.390
uninteresting. And then I get tweets or

00:00:39.390 --> 00:00:40.800
tumblr messages from people who are like

00:00:40.800 --> 00:00:43.379
Target store your giraffe love thing.

00:00:43.379 --> 00:00:45.180
No they didn't. A designer had the same

00:00:45.180 --> 00:00:47.190
idea that I did, that giraffes could make

00:00:47.190 --> 00:00:48.539
hearts with their necks, and I am more

00:00:48.539 --> 00:00:50.700
power to 'em, I'm all for artists making

00:00:50.700 --> 00:00:52.079
a living.  Moving into the past even

00:00:52.079 --> 00:00:54.510
further here, in 2007, John, your friend

00:00:54.510 --> 00:00:57.390
Katie Else put DFTBA on the end of her

00:00:57.390 --> 00:01:00.120
to-do list. We stole that from her

00:01:00.120 --> 00:01:02.370
and popularized it through this video blog

00:01:02.370 --> 00:01:05.070
and now DFTBA is a thing that I'm proud

00:01:05.070 --> 00:01:07.500
of and that I identify with and lots of

00:01:07.500 --> 00:01:10.320
people make DFTBA stuff. Some of those

00:01:10.320 --> 00:01:12.270
people are just crafty, createy

00:01:12.270 --> 00:01:14.640
people on Etsy, and some of them are big,

00:01:14.640 --> 00:01:16.350
global corporations. Are those people

00:01:16.350 --> 00:01:17.820
stealing from us? Are they stealing from

00:01:17.820 --> 00:01:19.619
Katie? Are they stealing from no one?

00:01:19.619 --> 00:01:21.240
Personally, I like it when it's stuff

00:01:21.240 --> 00:01:23.250
that I like, like this sewn greeting card

00:01:23.250 --> 00:01:25.680
by socutebykrista on Etsy, and then I

00:01:25.680 --> 00:01:27.270
don't like it when it's stuff that I

00:01:27.270 --> 00:01:29.159
don't really like like this shirt from

00:01:29.159 --> 00:01:31.320
Delia's that, also in addition to saying

00:01:31.320 --> 00:01:33.000
don't forget to be awesome, says keep

00:01:33.000 --> 00:01:36.119
calm and party on! Like one meme wasn't enough!

00:01:36.119 --> 00:01:37.979
But there's a difference between having

00:01:37.979 --> 00:01:40.350
a sense of ownership over something like

00:01:40.350 --> 00:01:43.619
I do over DFTBA, and actually owning it. I

00:01:43.619 --> 00:01:45.869
personally don't want to cave in to that

00:01:45.869 --> 00:01:47.790
desire for control. I have been able to

00:01:47.790 --> 00:01:49.439
create a really cool project based on

00:01:49.439 --> 00:01:51.659
the work of another creator, that creator

00:01:51.659 --> 00:01:53.610
being Jane Austen, who I do not put

00:01:53.610 --> 00:01:55.200
myself on the same level with, to be

00:01:55.200 --> 00:01:57.479
clear. And Walt Disney interestingly did

00:01:57.479 --> 00:01:59.430
the same thing with Alice in Wonderland,

00:01:59.430 --> 00:02:00.570
and The Little Mermaid, and Cinderella.

00:02:00.570 --> 00:02:02.880
Those were all stories before Disney

00:02:02.880 --> 00:02:04.890
came along and made them into movies. But now,

00:02:04.890 --> 00:02:07.380
Disney has turned, and is the primary force

00:02:07.380 --> 00:02:09.539
behind extending the copyright length

00:02:09.539 --> 00:02:10.600
forever.

00:02:10.600 --> 00:02:13.810
The good news is the internet is making all

00:02:13.810 --> 00:02:15.550
of this obsolete. Fewer and fewer

00:02:15.550 --> 00:02:17.440
corporations are enforcing copyright in

00:02:17.440 --> 00:02:18.640
the way that they used to,

00:02:18.640 --> 00:02:21.310
there's just too much derivative work

00:02:21.310 --> 00:02:21.910
going on.

00:02:21.910 --> 00:02:23.080
I remember when wizard-rock was first

00:02:23.080 --> 00:02:25.030
happening, and like the publishing companies

00:02:25.030 --> 00:02:26.500
behind Harry Potter were like "no, you

00:02:26.500 --> 00:02:28.240
can't make derivative works based on

00:02:28.240 --> 00:02:30.040
Harry Potter, you can't make Harry Potter

00:02:30.040 --> 00:02:32.530
songs". And yeah, you can. Why would you

00:02:32.530 --> 00:02:34.090
stop people from being enthusiastic

00:02:34.090 --> 00:02:36.430
about your creations? Hagh!

00:02:36.430 --> 00:02:39.250
Now when somebody posts a lip-sync

00:02:39.250 --> 00:02:46.150
video of One Direction, [lip synching to One Direction] the video doesn't get taken down.

00:02:46.150 --> 00:02:48.100
YouTube's algorithms identify it as a

00:02:48.100 --> 00:02:49.420
One Direction song,

00:02:49.420 --> 00:02:51.430
advertisement pop up on it, and Sony

00:02:51.430 --> 00:02:53.500
Music international gets the money from

00:02:53.500 --> 00:02:56.230
those advertisements. This is not law! In

00:02:56.230 --> 00:02:58.900
fact, it's not really legal. But the

00:02:58.900 --> 00:03:00.250
lip-sync person gets to keep having

00:03:00.250 --> 00:03:01.810
their video online, and One Direction

00:03:01.810 --> 00:03:03.370
gets to make money, and everybody's happy,

00:03:03.370 --> 00:03:05.920
so the law doesn't really come into it.

00:03:05.920 --> 00:03:09.190
It's kind of just corporations deciding what's

00:03:09.190 --> 00:03:12.190
ok and what isn't. Which is worrisome.

00:03:12.190 --> 00:03:14.230
Almost all creations before, you know,

00:03:14.230 --> 00:03:16.750
the 1700s, were collaborative creations.

00:03:16.750 --> 00:03:18.520
Like, a song could be written by dozens of

00:03:18.520 --> 00:03:20.080
different people over hundreds of years.

00:03:20.080 --> 00:03:23.440
And the internet is again, full of things

00:03:23.440 --> 00:03:24.040
like that.

00:03:24.040 --> 00:03:25.330
Creations that about making people happy

00:03:25.330 --> 00:03:26.800
or making them laugh or making them

00:03:26.800 --> 00:03:28.390
think or getting a reaction. Not

00:03:28.390 --> 00:03:30.760
about increasing personal wealth or

00:03:30.760 --> 00:03:32.740
controling creative vision. Ideas being

00:03:32.740 --> 00:03:34.900
free might not be good for the

00:03:34.900 --> 00:03:37.180
pocketbooks of individuals scattered

00:03:37.180 --> 00:03:39.670
around the world, but it is better for

00:03:39.670 --> 00:03:42.130
society and culture as a whole. And that's

00:03:42.130 --> 00:03:44.380
that's what makes life beautiful!

00:03:44.380 --> 00:03:46.660
Oh-oh! John, I'll see on Monday. I actually will be

00:03:46.660 --> 00:03:47.770
seeing John on Monday, that wasn't a

00:03:47.770 --> 00:03:49.120
screw-up, and I may be seeing you as well

00:03:49.120 --> 00:03:51.070
because we're having two pre-Carnegie

00:03:51.070 --> 00:03:52.990
rehearsal shows in New York. Tickets are

00:03:52.990 --> 00:03:53.680
ten dollars

00:03:53.680 --> 00:03:55.330
all proceeds go to charity link, in the

00:03:55.330 --> 00:04:01.260
description. Bye.

